      "writtenOn": "2020-01-31T21:21:28Z",
      "side": 1,
      "message": "From a 50,000\u0027 view, I think it\u0027s better to encode requirements in the API docs rather than VTS tests. \n\nAlso, I\u0027m not sure the VTS test is going to provide enough details... For example, the VTS test will not test the root of trust since this can either be Google\u0027s root cert or it could be some other authority. At the end of the day it comes down to what the Relying Party requires... the above list is just some kind of guess as to what we think RPs are going to want to see.\n\nAdditionally, the docs for this method starts out with stating \"must be generated using Keymaster Attestation\" and that\u0027s why the somewhat standard stuff like osVersion, rootOfTrust (with the VerifiedBootState) isn\u0027t listed since I\u0027m assuming it\u0027s covered by that provision... the rationale for this is that I don\u0027t want to duplicate requirements.\n\nWe should probably have the VTS test test that there _is_ a rootOfTrust though (even if it\u0027s not explicitly listed here)... but I don\u0027t think we need to list it here since the approach is \"Use KM attestation, with these differences\".",
      "parentUuid": "4da470d9_91aaeda5",
      "range": {
        "startLine": 61,
        "startChar": 16,
        "endLine": 61,
        "endChar": 52
      },
